# /etc/ufw/after.rules — DOCKER-USER chain integration fragment.
#
# Round 3 PR-B (edge security hardening). Closes Round 3 audit
# A07-P0-002 partial: re-enable UFW without breaking Docker.
#
# THE PROBLEM
# -----------
# Docker manages its own iptables rules in the DOCKER and DOCKER-USER chains.
# These rules apply BEFORE UFW's INPUT/FORWARD chains for traffic destined
# to Docker-published ports — which means a vanilla UFW config either:
#   (a) does nothing about Docker-published ports (UFW rules ignored), OR
#   (b) breaks all Docker traffic if FORWARD policy is set to DROP.
#
# THE FIX (chaifeng/ufw-docker pattern)
# -------------------------------------
# Append the block below to /etc/ufw/after.rules, BEFORE the final COMMIT
# line. This:
#   - Whitelists private RFC1918 ranges (containers can talk to each other
#     and to the host LAN)
#   - Allows DNS replies (UDP src=53)
#   - Hands control to a `ufw-user-forward` chain so UFW rules apply
#   - Drops outside attempts to reach private RFC1918 from the public net
#     (defense-in-depth — Docker shouldn't be exposing those, but if a
#     misconfig ever does, this catches it)
#
# ALTERNATIVE: install via upstream tool
# --------------------------------------
#   curl -fsSL https://github.com/chaifeng/ufw-docker/raw/master/ufw-docker \
#     -o /usr/local/bin/ufw-docker
#   chmod +x /usr/local/bin/ufw-docker
#   ufw-docker install
# This appends the SAME rules to /etc/ufw/after.rules. The fragment below
# is what `ufw-docker install` produces — provided here for repo audit
# trail and for environments where the upstream curl is undesirable.
#
# WHERE TO PASTE
# --------------
# /etc/ufw/after.rules already has this structure:
#
#   #
#   # rules.input-after
#   # ...
#   *filter
#   :ufw-after-input - [0:0]
#   ...
#   COMMIT      <-- BEFORE this final COMMIT, paste the block below.
#
# After pasting, run:
#   sudo ufw reload
#   sudo iptables -L DOCKER-USER -n   # verify the chain is populated

# === BEGIN ufw-docker DOCKER-USER fragment ===

# Allow forwarded traffic to/from Docker private subnets (the docker0 bridge
# and any user-defined bridge networks docker-compose creates).
:DOCKER-USER - [0:0]
:ufw-user-forward - [0:0]

# Container-to-container (RFC1918) — allow.
-A DOCKER-USER -j RETURN -s 10.0.0.0/8
-A DOCKER-USER -j RETURN -s 172.16.0.0/12
-A DOCKER-USER -j RETURN -s 192.168.0.0/16

# DNS replies (clients made the query, server replies on src 53).
-A DOCKER-USER -p udp -m udp --sport 53 --dport 1024:65535 -j RETURN

# Hand off to the UFW user-rule chain so `ufw allow 80/tcp` etc. apply to
# Docker-published ports.
-A DOCKER-USER -j ufw-user-forward

# Defense-in-depth: drop SYNs from the public net to RFC1918 destinations.
# A correctly-configured docker-compose never exposes a private subnet
# externally, but a future misconfig could; these rules block it.
-A DOCKER-USER -j DROP -p tcp -m tcp --tcp-flags FIN,SYN,RST,ACK SYN -d 192.168.0.0/16
-A DOCKER-USER -j DROP -p tcp -m tcp --tcp-flags FIN,SYN,RST,ACK SYN -d 10.0.0.0/8
-A DOCKER-USER -j DROP -p tcp -m tcp --tcp-flags FIN,SYN,RST,ACK SYN -d 172.16.0.0/12

# Same for UDP (avoid DNS amplification reflectors).
-A DOCKER-USER -j DROP -p udp -m udp --dport 0:32767 -d 192.168.0.0/16
-A DOCKER-USER -j DROP -p udp -m udp --dport 0:32767 -d 10.0.0.0/8
-A DOCKER-USER -j DROP -p udp -m udp --dport 0:32767 -d 172.16.0.0/12

# === END ufw-docker DOCKER-USER fragment ===
#
# After pasting and reloading UFW, verify Docker compose containers can
# still:
#   - Reach each other over their compose network (postgres ↔ transcribe-md)
#   - Reach the public net for outbound API calls (Anthropic, Deepgram, …)
#   - Be reached by the host on their published ports (curl localhost:3001)
#
# Expected `ufw status verbose` after rule + this fragment:
#   Status: active
#   Logging: on (low)
#   Default: deny (incoming), allow (outgoing), disabled (routed)
#   To                         Action      From
#   --                         ------      ----
#   22/tcp                     ALLOW IN    Anywhere
#   80/tcp                     ALLOW IN    Anywhere
#   443/tcp                    ALLOW IN    Anywhere
#
# If Docker breaks after enabling UFW, IMMEDIATELY run:
#   sudo ufw disable
# then debug. Do NOT leave production broken trying to fix forward.
